This checklist outlines the standards for responsible and professional social media behavior as a university student
 The Do's
Audit your privacy settings: Check who can see your personal posts, pictures, and campus locations tagged by your friends.
Before sharing your post, think about the future internship or job opportunities that may be affected by your post.
Showcase your skills: Share your academic achievements and your programming skills and design portfolios on the Internet.
Before sharing news and campus announcements, verify the information.
Network: Reach out to alumni and professors with proper etiquette and polite language.
 The Don'ts
Don't post unprofessional content: Never post pictures that involve the use of offensive language and damage to campus property.
Don't fall into the trap of Internet trolls: Never engage with trolls in the comments section of your post; it's better to ignore them.
Don't share your personal information: Never post pictures of your college ID cards, your room numbers, and your passwords.
Don't use university logos and branding without permission: Never create an "official" page or group with the university's logo without the permission of the faculty members.
Don't vent about your professors: If you have a grievance against your professors, never post it on the Internet and rather use the official university email for the same.
